course: dictionary
lesson: 12432
title: The deceiver
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A deceiver is a person who deceives. To deceive means to trick people into believing something that is not true. So a deceiver is someone who tricks others, often with lies.
explain: Look at the words around a new word to find its meaning. «The stranger promised free sweets to every child, but no sweets ever appeared. He was a deceiver.» The promise was false, so a deceiver must be someone who tricks people with false promises.
explain: Stories use deceivers to warn the reader. When a character is called a deceiver, do not trust what he says. Ask yourself: is this character telling the truth, or is he tricking someone?
fact: Deceiver is a noun. One deceiver, two deceivers.
fact: To deceive means to make someone believe something false.
fact: «Deceiver» is often used in stories, fables and the Bible, where it warns the reader not to trust a character.
fact: Everyday words with a similar meaning are liar, cheat and trickster.
fact: Deceivers can seem friendly or honest at first, so readers should check what they really do.
exercise: «The fox told the hens the gate was open, but it was a trap.» What is the fox? | *A deceiver | A farmer | A gardener | A hen
exercise: Which word means a person who tricks people into believing something false? | Builder | Gardener | Painter | *Deceiver
exercise: «Tom said his bag was empty, but he had hidden the cake inside it.» Why is Tom a deceiver? | He told the truth about the cake | *He tricked people into believing something false | He hid his bag under the table | He baked the cake himself
test: «The salesman promised the car had no faults, but it broke down on the first day.» What does deceiver mean? | Someone who builds cars | A type of car | Someone who always tells the truth | *Someone who tricks people into believing something false
test: Which word is closest in meaning to deceiver? | *Trickster | Judge | Helper | Painter
exam: The fable warns us: «Do not trust the deceiver, however sweet his words.» What is the fable warning us about? | To be kind to strangers | To read more books | To buy more sweets | *Not to trust a person who tricks us
